From 2f22028fb3be78f945f60a0dfb9901b856a41258 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Attard <sam@electronjs.org>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 18:58:48 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix: stop a hidden page rendering after
 setBackgroundThrottling is re-enabled (#54311)

setBackgroundThrottling(false) on a page nobody can see shows its
RenderWidgetHost as hidden-but-painting, and disable_hidden_ swallows any
hide that arrives while throttling is off. setBackgroundThrottling(true)
only cleared the flag, and content re-hides the widget only on the next
visibility transition, so a window that was already hidden, occluded or
minimized kept producing frames at full rate until its visibility next
changed. Re-hide the widgets (the main frame's and any cross-process
iframe's, which HandleNewRenderFrame also stamps) when throttling is
re-enabled and the page's computed visibility is hidden, and show the
child-frame widgets alongside the main one when it is disabled.

The renderer had the same gap: the page was forced to kVisible while
throttling was off and only picked its real visibility up again at the next
lifecycle update, so document.visibilityState stayed 'visible' and timers
stayed unthrottled. WebViewImpl now remembers the visibility the browser
last asked for and re-applies it when SetSchedulerThrottling flips, and the
forced-visible state is reported to WebViewObservers too so media does not
stay background-paused on a page that is otherwise treated as visible.
---
 ..._scheduler_throttling_per_renderview.patch |  42 ++++--
 .../browser/api/electron_api_web_contents.cc  |  45 ++++++-
 spec/api-web-contents.spec.ts                 | 124 ++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 195 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

